<article class="env-definition" id="058I">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/058I">Definition <span data-tag="058I">10.82.1</span>.</a> Let $f: M \to N$ be a map of $R$-modules. Then $f$ is called <em>universally injective</em> if for every $R$-module $Q$, the map $f \otimes _ R \text{id}_ Q: M \otimes _ R Q \to N \otimes _ R Q$ is injective. A sequence $0 \to M_1 \to M_2 \to M_3 \to 0$ of $R$-modules is called <em>universally exact</em> if it is exact and $M_1 \to M_2$ is universally injective. </p>
</article>